Abstract

Analyses of intersection accidents have shown that in most cases drivers overlook or see other road users with the right of way too late that they cannot react in time. Appropriate visual warning signals which support drivers in their attention allocation and driving behavior could be useful to improve drivers’ reaction. In a driving simulator study, two warning strategies varying in their timing: (1) top-down warning while approaching a critical intersection and (2) bottom-up warning directly before the critical incident were investigated in a T-intersection situation. For the bottom-up warning, two warning signal designs were compared. 48 subjects ( M  = 27.3 years, SD = 7.4 years) participated in the study. Driving data as well as subjective evaluation of the three warning signals (one early and two late warning signals) were analyzed. The early warning signal which was given while approaching the intersection showed a positive effect. Here, most collisions could be avoided due to drivers’ adaptation of their driving behavior toward safer driving. They waited longer at the intersection before turning and finally turned with a lower velocity. In addition, drivers evaluated the early warning signal as very “useful”. With regard to the late warnings a much smaller effect was found. From these results, requirements can be derived for the design of effective warning strategies when driving at critical intersections.
Highlights

► In most intersection accidents, drivers see other road users with the right of way too late to react in time. ► Appropriate warnings which support drivers in their driving behavior and reaction could be useful. ► Two visual warning strategies varied in their timing (while approaching and near a critical incident) were investigated at a T-intersection. ► Most collisions could be avoided with the early warning due to longer waiting time and lower velocity while turning. ► Requirements can be derived for the design of effective warning strategies when driving in critical intersection situations.
Introduction

Driving at intersections is one of the most dynamic and complex task of drivers. Depending on the intersection situation (e.g. intersection type, driving maneuver, and traffic volume), drivers have to perceive different kinds of information by shifting their attention to various task-relevant objects, e.g. crossing vehicles, vehicles driving ahead and oncoming, traffic signs, as well as pedestrians and cyclists. For safe driving, a high degree of visual and spatial processing is required (Hills, 1980; see also, Cummings, 1986, Moore, 1969). In-depth analyses of car accidents have found that perceptual errors which are also called recognition errors (Rumar, 1990) are one of the major contributing factors for accidents at intersections (see, e.g. Clayton, 1976, Fell, 1976, Sabey and Staughton, 1975, Staughton and Storie, 1977, Treat et al., 1979, Vollrath et al., 2006). For example, an in-depth accident survey in Germany (Vollrath et al., 2006) found that in above 90% of intersection accidents lack of information was the main reason leading to an accident. In most cases, drivers failed to yield to other traffic participants with the right of way as they were not noticed by the drivers. Other accident studies have shown similar results (see, e.g. Graab et al., 2008, Gründl, 2005, Hoppe et al., 2007, Wierwille and Tijerina, 1995).

In most of the intersection accidents mentioned above, drivers overlooked other traffic participants (“Looked-but-failed-to-see accidents”; see Brown, 2005, Cairney and Catchpole, 1996, Herslund and Jørgensen, 2003, Koustanaï et al., 2008, Langham et al., 2002, Treat, 1980, White and Caird, 2010) or failed to look at the right place and thus saw the other traffic participant too late to react in time. Especially, according to different in-depth studies in the Nordic countries (see, e.g. Pasanen, 1992, Räsänen and Summala, 1998, Räsänen and Summala, 2000, Summala et al., 1996) this last kind of perceptual error was found to be relevant in bicycle-car accidents. According to the authors, improper visual scanning strategies and unjustified expectations for events and hazards by drivers could be responsible for this.

With regard to the second accident type (“failed to look”), a recent driving simulator study by Werneke and Vollrath (2012) confirmed these results. The aim of the study was to investigate drivers’ attention allocation and driving behavior in different T-intersection situations which varied in traffic volume and the presence or absence of pedestrians. Additionally, it was examined how well drivers could react to a critical event presented in these different intersection situations. Results of the study showed that, first with regard to the intersection situation drivers allocated their attention mainly to certain areas of the intersection where they expected task-relevant objects. In contrast, other areas were rather neglected by them.

Second, their velocity when turning differed depending on the intersection situation. Especially, at intersections with a low traffic volume and no pedestrians they drove with the highest velocity. This resulted in many collisions when a critical event occurred. In contrast, drivers could best avoid a collision when they allocated their attention more properly at another type of intersection and turned right with a lower velocity.

In conclusion, there are certain intersection situations in which the probability of an accident is systematically higher because of drivers’ improper attention allocation and driving behavior. As this is the case, one can try to prevent these accidents by using appropriate driver warning systems which support drivers in their attention allocation and driving behavior.

In the last decade, a lot of effort was put into the development of intersection assistance systems (see, e.g. Doerzaph et al., 2007, Hopstock et al., 2005, Mages, 2008; to mention just a few) and into the design of appropriate human–machine-interface concepts (see, e.g. Benmimoun et al., 2007, Campbell et al., 2007, Klanner et al., 2008, Thoma et al., 2008). However, there have only been few studies which systematically compared different strategies which aim at supporting drivers in the most appropriate manner (e.g. Lee et al., 2009). One warning strategy could be to try to inform drivers at an early stage about a potential critical event at the intersection so that drivers can adapt their attention and driving behavior well in advance in a manner that enables them to better react when a critical situation occurs. The second warning strategy could be to warn drivers immediately before the critical event so that they can react faster when seeing the critical object.

The aim of the first warning strategy can be described as trying to change drivers’ expectations about the current driving situation so that they are prepared for the situation and can adapt their attention and driving behavior accordingly. From a technical point of view this requires knowledge about future events or about traffic participants which are not directly visible for the driver. Car-to-x communication technologies which are currently being developed could provide this kind of information (see, e.g. Festag et al., 2008, Franz, 2004, Lübke, 2004, Röglinger, 2011, Röglinger and Facchi, 2009).

With regard to the second warning strategy, one would try to direct drivers’ attention toward the critical event in order to elicit a fast and effective reaction. One could present warning signals in the focus of drivers’ view (e.g. in a head-up display) so that the warning is easily perceived and drivers are enabled to react quite fast. One could also try to present salient and powerful warning signals in drivers’ peripheral vision which attract their attention in an automatic manner (see, e.g. Egeth and Yantis, 1997, Remington et al., 1992, Ruz and Lupiánez, 2002, Theeuwes, 1992, Theeuwes, 2004, Theeuwes and Godijn, 2001, Yantis and Egeth, 1999, Yantis and Jonides, 1990). This would disrupt the current attention allocation strategy of drivers and would direct their attention toward the appropriate intersection area so the unexpected hazard can be perceived faster. As a result, drivers can react faster. Additionally, this does not require conscious awareness by drivers and does not reduce their capacity for performing other tasks (see, e.g. Jonides, 1981, Posner and Snyder, 1975).

First studies have already demonstrated the positive effect of such peripheral warning signals (see, e.g. Arnon, 2010, Falkmer et al., 2000, Fricke, 2009, Henning et al., 2008, Ho and Spence, 2009, Kienast et al., 2008, Laquai et al., 2010, Mahlke et al., 2007, Victor and Jarlengrip, 2005). In most cases, peripheral warning signals were examined in context of pedestrian detection by drivers at night (see, e.g. Henning et al., 2008, Kienast et al., 2008, Mahlke et al., 2007). While these peripheral in-vehicle warning signals have been shown to be effective, it is still unclear how these signals should be best designed and what are the most appropriate signals especially in complex intersection situations where there is already a lot of information presented for drivers.

To summarize, theoretical considerations suggest that two different warning strategies might be effective to prevent intersection accidents where drivers overlook other traffic participants or see them too late. On the one hand, an early warning could enable drivers to adapt their attention and behavior in a manner that critical objects are better detected and drivers are able to react faster. On the other hand, later warnings presented concurrently with the appearance of critical objects could support drivers to perceive these objects and react faster. These late warnings could either presented in the drivers’ central view which ensures that the warning can be perceived very well by them. However, drivers would then still have the problem of perceiving the critical object. In order to facilitate this, one could also present peripheral warnings which are able to attract drivers’ attention toward the critical object.

As expectations allow drivers to adapt their behaviors, one would expect the warning strategy (first warning strategy) to be most effective. However, it is not always feasible to have this kind of information well in advance. Thus, the second warning strategy (late warning strategy) should also be examined. As it takes time to shift the attention one would expect that peripheral warnings should be more effective as warnings presented in the center of drivers’ view. These peripheral warnings lead to an automatic and very fast shift of attention. In addition, it should take some additional time to understand a warning which is presented in the middle of the field of view.

In line with these considerations, three warning signals were compared in the present study. To this aim, an unsignalized T-intersection situation was used in which drivers had to turn right and where a critical event happened just after drivers had begun their turn. The three warning signals used in the study are described in detail in the next section.
